{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Information.MagnitudeAxisBoundary","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Information.MagnitudeAxisBoundary","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Fin","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.census_separates_on_magnitude","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.census_separates_on_magnitude","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/MagnitudeAxisBoundary.lean#L80-L87","doc":"**The census DOES separate on the magnitude axis (sorry-free).**  Rounding\nout the boundary: when the accepting-counts differ, the census — and hence\nany injective magnitude readout — separates.  So magnitude invariants are\nexactly the census-difference detectors: powerful on their own axis, blind\noff it.  Concrete witness: the all-reject vs all-accept predicates. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.census_blind_to_decision","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.census_blind_to_decision","kind":"theorem","line":63,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/MagnitudeAxisBoundary.lean#L63-L78","doc":"**The counting/decision boundary (sorry-free, non-vacuous).**  There is a\ngenuinely different decision pair — `p ≠ q` as predicates — on which\n*every* census-factoring functional agrees.  In particular the surreal\nbirthday of the accepting-count cannot distinguish them: it lives on the\nmagnitude axis, the difference lives on the predicate axis.  This is the\n`#P`-vs-decision wall as one Lean statement — the exact reason the\n`SurrealBudgetGrade` magnitude squeeze does not, by itself, yield\n`P≠NP`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.factorsThroughCensus_comp_census","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.factorsThroughCensus_comp_census","kind":"theorem","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/MagnitudeAxisBoundary.lean#L55-L61","doc":"**The birthday-of-count functional factors through the census\n(sorry-free).**  For *any* `b : ℕ → α`, `b ∘ census` is a magnitude\nfunctional — the surreal birthday `b(census p)` is the intended `b`.  So\nthe boundary below applies to the birthday. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.FactorsThroughCensus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.FactorsThroughCensus","kind":"def","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/MagnitudeAxisBoundary.lean#L49-L53","doc":"**A magnitude functional is anything that factors through the census.**\n`F` sees only *how many* witnesses accept, not *which*.  The surreal\nbirthday `b ∘ census` is the guiding instance. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.census","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.census","kind":"def","line":43,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/MagnitudeAxisBoundary.lean#L43-L47","doc":"**Census (the universal magnitude functional).**  The number of accepting\nwitnesses of a decision predicate `p : Fin k → Bool`.  Every magnitude /\nsimplicity invariant — the ω-grade, and the **surreal birthday of the\ncount** — factors through this natural number. ","signatureChunk":0}]}